+## When `beforeSession` itself throws
+
+A deployer that allocates in `beforeSession` — a span, a database handle, a rate-limit token — and releases it in `afterSession` needs the release on exactly the path where the allocation failed, so a throw from `beforeSession` is a turn failure like any other and the hooks that serve turn failures serve this one.
+
+- A throw from `beforeSession` reaches the caller as a rejection carrying the thrown error, `onError` runs first with the same `(session, error)` arguments any other orchestrator failure delivers, and `afterSession` runs after it ([validated by: a throwing beforeSession runs onError then afterSession and still rejects with the thrown error](../../src/orchestration/chatOrchestrator.acceptance.test.ts#L28)).
+- On that path none of the turn has run: the `session` `afterSession` receives carries exactly the entries it carried when the call began, and the provider has not been called, so a cleanup hook reading what it is handed can tell a turn that never started from one that failed part-way ([validated by: hands afterSession a session the turn never touched](../../src/orchestration/chatOrchestrator.acceptance.test.ts#L63)).
